SMPP — Interview Notes

────────────────────────────────────────
Q: What is SMPP?

SMPP = **Short Message Peer-to-Peer** protocol.

It is an open protocol used to send/receive **SMS** between:
- Your application (ESME — External Short Messaging Entity)
- SMSC (Short Message Service Center) of a telecom / SMS aggregator

Think:
Your Spring Boot service  ←SMPP→  SMS provider / telecom SMSC  →  user’s phone

Not an HTTP API by itself (though many modern providers also offer HTTP REST for SMS).
SMPP is the classic, high-throughput, binary TCP protocol for SMS.

────────────────────────────────────────
Why use SMPP?

| Reason            | Explanation                                      |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| High volume SMS   | Designed for bulk / continuous SMS traffic       |
| Persistent link   | Keep a TCP bind open; send many messages         |
| Delivery reports  | Get DLR (delivered / failed / expired, etc.)     |
| Industry standard | Telecom / aggregators still widely use it        |

When HTTP SMS APIs are enough: low volume, simple integrations.
When SMPP shines: thousands of SMS/sec, telecom-grade control, DLR streaming.

────────────────────────────────────────
Core concepts

| Term   | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| ESME   | Your app that binds to SMSC |
| SMSC   | Message center that routes SMS to mobile network |
| Bind   | Login/session to SMSC (like opening a channel) |
| PDU    | Protocol Data Unit — SMPP message packet |
| DLR    | Delivery receipt / delivery report |

Bind types:
- **BIND_TRANSMITTER** — send SMS only
- **BIND_RECEIVER** — receive SMS / DLRs only
- **BIND_TRANSCEIVER** — send + receive on same session (common)

────────────────────────────────────────
High-level flow (submit SMS)

1. App opens TCP connection to SMSC host:port
2. Send **bind** (system_id, password, bind type)
3. SMSC accepts bind
4. App sends **submit_sm** (from, to, text, optional params)
5. SMSC replies **submit_sm_resp** (message_id)
6. Later SMSC may send **deliver_sm** as DLR (status of that message_id)
7. On shutdown: **unbind**

Conceptual:

Spring Boot App (ESME)
        │  TCP + SMPP
        ▼
     SMSC / Aggregator
        │
        ▼
   Mobile Network → Phone

────────────────────────────────────────
Important PDUs (know names for interviews)

| PDU              | Role                              |
| ---------------- | --------------------------------- |
| bind_*           | Start session                     |
| submit_sm        | Send MT (mobile-terminated) SMS   |
| submit_sm_resp   | Ack + message id                  |
| deliver_sm       | MO SMS or DLR from SMSC           |
| enquire_link     | Keepalive / heartbeat             |
| unbind           | Close session                     |

────────────────────────────────────────
Java / Spring Boot angle

SMPP is not built into Spring Boot.
You use a library + connection management, e.g.:
- OpenSMPP
- jsmpp
- cloudhopper-smpp (common historically)

Typical design in a microservice:

SMS API / Order Service
        ↓
   SMS Outbox / Queue (Kafka/Rabbit)
        ↓
   SMPP Gateway service (keeps bind alive)
        ↓
   SMSC

Why separate gateway?
- One long-lived SMPP session (or pool)
- Rate limits, retries, DLR handling
- Don’t open/close bind per HTTP request

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview tips / pitfalls

- Bind is expensive → reuse session; use enquire_link to keep alive
- Handle reconnect if SMSC drops TCP
- Encode correctly (GSM-7 vs UCS-2 for Unicode/Arabic/Urdu, etc.)
- Long SMS → segmentation (UDH / message parts)
- Idempotency + outbox if “send SMS” must not duplicate on retry
- Secure credentials; prefer private network / VPN to SMSC
- Distinguish **submit success** (accepted by SMSC) vs **delivered to phone** (DLR)

────────────────────────────────────────
SMPP vs HTTP SMS API

| Point        | SMPP                         | HTTP SMS API            |
| ------------ | ---------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Transport    | TCP binary PDUs              | REST/JSON over HTTP     |
| Session      | Persistent bind              | Usually request/response|
| Throughput   | Very high                    | Often lower / rate-limited differently |
| Complexity   | Higher                       | Easier                  |
| DLR          | Streaming deliver_sm         | Webhooks / poll         |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Short Interview Answer

SMPP is the Short Message Peer-to-Peer protocol used to exchange SMS with an SMSC.
Our app binds as an ESME over TCP, submits messages with submit_sm, and can receive delivery reports via deliver_sm.
In Spring Boot we typically run a dedicated SMS gateway service using a library like jsmpp/cloudhopper, 
keeping a persistent bind for high-volume, reliable SMS — unlike simple one-off HTTP SMS APIs.
